WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:04.380
(easy listening music
and children playing)

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.570
- When I was working
as a registered nurse,

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:10.000
I was also traveling to many
different parts of the world.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.880
And that's where I realized
that these hospitals

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.260
don't have any medical supplies
to take care of patients.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.550
And that's really when I said to myself,

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.610
I'm going to figure this problem out.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.110
And that's what I've done.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.490
My name is Elizabeth McLellan,
and I'm the president

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.540
and founder of Partners for World Health.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.453
Our healthcare system is
extremely complicated.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:40.620
There are many federal rules
and regulations in our country.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.760
And as a result of that,
all healthcare centers

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.290
have to discard medical supplies
because they may have been

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:48.493
in a patient's room.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.850
These are not used medical supplies.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.930
They are clean, they are unopened,

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.820
and could be used in the developing world.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.330
I started collecting
medical supplies in 2007.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:03.960
And because there was
no place to store them

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.570
in the healthcare facilities,
I started to take them home.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.840
My cellar filled up from
the floor to the ceiling,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.170
and it overflowed into
the downstairs bathroom

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:15.160
until there was just a little
path that I could follow.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.020
And then, eventually,
in September of 2009,

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.690
I put my money in to
rent the first warehouse.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.650
And there are so many people
that have joined with us

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.200
on this to keep it going.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.623
So it has a life of its own
now, which is pretty exciting.

00:01:32.272 --> 00:01:33.540
(upbeat chamber music)

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.240
We load the containers
with 15 to 18,000 pounds

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.163
of discarded medical supplies.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.770
We collect these discarded
supplies now from all over

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.070
the state of Maine,
New Hampshire, Vermont,

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.473
and 15 hospitals in Massachusetts.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.690
And we bring them up here to
our distribution warehouse

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:55.970
where we sort through
all of these supplies.

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.640
We package them, check expiration dates,

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.380
and we ship the container loads
over to different countries

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.040
in Asia and Africa, the
Middle East, all over India

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.223
and Pakistan, Sri Lanka, et cetera.

00:02:09.722 --> 00:02:10.650
(soft piano music)

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.670
After the hurricane in Puerto Rico,

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.940
we identified that there
was a huge need down there.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.600
And so we are shipping
the fourth container

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.633
of medical supplies to be
distributed in Puerto Rico.

00:02:57.998 --> 00:03:00.248
(laughter)

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.570
- We have a model that works,
so there's a way to do this.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:09.100
If I can collect one
bag of medical supplies,

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:10.950
now look what's happened.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.830
And so it's important that
we recognize that there are

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.960
so many people in our
world that would benefit

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.530
from these discarded medical supplies.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:23.440
So, that's why I'm doing this.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.680
Because it's very important
to me that we try to reach out

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.930
and be committed to something greater than

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:33.383
our own self interests and
make a difference in our world.

00:03:34.618 --> 00:03:38.118
(flowing orchestra music)

